WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:04.880
Imagine that you are not watching&nbsp;
the microcosmos right now.

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:11.640
Instead, imagine you are living in the world as&nbsp;
it existed around one billion years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:15.720
and you are the ancestor of this red algae.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:21.480
Inside of you lies the DNA that you have&nbsp;
painstakingly acquired from your ancestors.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:25.240
It contains the instructions for everything from
the walls of your cellular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:28.000
body to the components of your organelles.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:34.360
That DNA has changed and accumulated through&nbsp;
randomness and selection over billions of years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:39.800
and you are the masterpiece&nbsp;
sculpted by that process.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:44.210
Except you are about to lose&nbsp;
a quarter of your genes.

00:00:58.187 --> 00:01:02.480
The history of red algae can be told&nbsp;
in part through what they have gained,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:05.960
lost, and given away over billions of years.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:10.160
It begins with a tale that is familiar&nbsp;
to many of us in the microcosmos.

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:14.720
A long, long, long time ago, a eukaryote consumed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:20.920
a cyanobacterium, only to convert it into a&nbsp;
photosynthetic organelle called a plastid.

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:26.480
This process is known as endosymbiosis, and&nbsp;
the organisms descended from that eukaryote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:30.960
are now known as the Archaeplastida,&nbsp;
which is made up of three groups.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:36.000
Green algae and land plants are&nbsp;
considered one of those groups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.280
and then there’s the other group&nbsp;
of algae called the glaucophytes.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.720
And then there are the red&nbsp;
algae, called rhodophyta.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:47.600
Like their Archaeplastid counterparts,&nbsp;
red algae have chlorophyll.

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:53.460
But they also possess other pigments that help&nbsp;
with photosynthesis and also often turn them red.

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:57.600
Now when you hear the word 
“algae,” your mind might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:01.400
first conjure up images of&nbsp;
the greener Archaeplastids.

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:04.960
But red algae is actually&nbsp;
widespread and influential.

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:09.749
For starters, if you eat nori,&nbsp;
you are actually eating red algae.

00:02:09.749 --> 00:02:14.080
And red algae’s impact goes far&nbsp;
beyond that. As we've just said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:17.920
red algae got their photosynthetic&nbsp;
plastids through endosymbiosis.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:23.605
But they also essentially gave their&nbsp;
plastids to others through endosymbiosis.

00:02:23.605 --> 00:02:29.480
Many organisms, like diatoms, can trace the&nbsp;
origins of their photosynthetic capabilities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:37.000
to an ancestor that consumed and converted&nbsp;
a red algae plastid into its own machinery.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:41.640
So red algae have a legacy that&nbsp;
spans far beyond their own actions.

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:45.840
They show up embedded in the ways&nbsp;
that other organisms are able to live,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:53.523
in every bit of food they derive from the sun&nbsp;
and the chemical byproducts that sustain us all.

00:02:53.523 --> 00:02:57.560
But for all that red algae were able to&nbsp;
gain and share through endosymbiosis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:02.080
their history is also marked&nbsp;
by the things they’ve lost.

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:04.197
Like their genes.

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:11.240
One of the things that is 
really striking about red&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:16.960
algae is they don’t seem to have that&nbsp;
many genes compared to other eukaryotes.

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:21.320
And more weirdly, it seems like they&nbsp;
actually once did have more genes…

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:23.760
they just lost them.

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:27.440
It’s hard to pick apart exactly&nbsp;
how red algae lost their genes.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:30.160
And we can’t say if it happened very suddenly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:33.800
or if it was more a gradual chipping&nbsp;
away at their genomes.

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:37.400
But based on the species&nbsp;
they’re able to study today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:43.528
scientists think there were at least&nbsp;
two major phases of genome reduction.

00:03:44.424 --> 00:03:51.560
The first likely involved some old lineage of&nbsp;
red algae, which likely lost about 25% of their genes.

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:56.320
And in this process, they lost&nbsp;
a key feature: the flagellum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:01.200
that thread-like structure that many&nbsp;
eukaryotes use to drive themselves around.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:05.760
And there are some interesting implications&nbsp;
to losing the flagellum, especially for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:11.160
an aquatic organism like red algae that would&nbsp;
probably want to be able to swim to locations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:16.360
that allow them better access to light,&nbsp;
or to find other algae to reproduce with.

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:20.600
But perhaps that ancestral red algae&nbsp;
was living in conditions where the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:25.800
energy required to make flagella and&nbsp;
operate them just wasn't worth it.

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:31.080
Maybe it just thrived without the flagellum,&nbsp;
and there never was a reason to keep it.

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:36.120
In that case, that ancestral red algae’s&nbsp;
loss seems to have been justified.

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:40.480
Because red algae are thriving without&nbsp;
their flagella, and without any number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:43.640
of other pathways that were lost with those genes.

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:50.080
And in fact, in the second phase, another red&nbsp;
algae would reduce its genome by another 25%.

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:55.240
The descendants of that algae became&nbsp;
members of a group known as Cyanidiophytina.

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:56.560
The algae within this group—

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:00.000
and particularly those from&nbsp;
the class Cyanidiophyceae—

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.680
are particularly well-studied&nbsp;
because they are extremophiles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:08.040
meaning they are found in environments&nbsp;
that are incredibly difficult to survive in.

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:10.320
Heat? Acid? Metal? Salt?

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:13.880
All things we might tolerate&nbsp;
in a very specific range,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:17.760
but these red algae extremophiles are not phased.

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:23.200
Some can survive in acidic environments&nbsp;
whose pH values range from 0 to 4.

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:28.520
For context, the least painful end of that&nbsp;
scale would be like living in acid rain.

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:29.960
The most painful?

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:31.560
Like battery acid.

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:33.000
And as far as temperature goes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:39.720
there are species that can survive at 56&nbsp;
degrees Celsius, or 132 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:41.600
So the reason we’re not actually featuring any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:47.520
Cyanidiophytes is that they tend to be&nbsp;
found in places like volcanos or hot springs.

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:53.680
No, our red algae are their tamer cousins,&nbsp;
found living along a Spanish beach.

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:55.720
But how could the Cyanidiophytes have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.720 --> 00:06:01.640
survived such extreme conditions when&nbsp;
they have lost so much of their genome?

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:05.093
Surely those lost genes were useful for something?

00:06:05.093 --> 00:06:06.000
Maybe.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:09.320
But it turns out the&nbsp;
cyanidiophytes had a back-up plan:

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:12.814
they could get the genes they&nbsp;
needed from their neighbors.

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:18.360
There’s a process scientists have&nbsp;
been unraveling over the past few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:25.059
decades called horizontal gene transfer, where&nbsp;
genetic material gets moved from one species

00:06:25.059 --> 00:06:26.120
to another.

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:29.920
And in general, we know it as a&nbsp;
process that happens in prokaryotes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:37.240
driven by viruses or other vectors that are able&nbsp;
to transfer DNA from one organism to another.

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:42.480
And prokaryotes are well-suited to not&nbsp;
only take in genetic material this way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:48.400
but also to quickly embed those changes in&nbsp;
their population as they divide asexually.

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:52.280
Eukaryotes seemed unlikely to&nbsp;
engage in horizontal gene transfer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:55.960
if only because we couldn’t&nbsp;
readily find examples of it.

00:06:55.960 --> 00:07:00.760
But when scientists studied the&nbsp;
genome of Cyanidiophyceae algae,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:06.120
they found hundreds of genes in them that seemed&nbsp;
to come from bacteria, and that seemed to help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:11.680
with various functions like surviving&nbsp;
high temperatures or salty conditions.

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:17.400
And so the same group of organisms that&nbsp;
lost so much of itself also found a way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:24.800
to gain so much of others, adopting the genes of&nbsp;
other organisms into their own way of life.

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:29.080
And the most exciting thing is how&nbsp;
much there is still to learn.

00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:32.800
We still really don’t know that&nbsp;
much about how horizontal gene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:35.880
transfer shows up in the history of eukaryotes.

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:41.000
We don’t know whether red algae have gone&nbsp;
through other major phases of genome reduction.

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:48.396
We know simply that nature’s boundaries are&nbsp;
porous, and its identities are multi-faceted.

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:58.040
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as&nbsp;
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:07:58.040 --> 00:08:02.240
The people on your screen right now, they are&nbsp;
